(Client-side sign-up page then calls `signIn("credentials", ...)` after this succeeds — see below.)

---

## 2. Auth library

- **next-auth v5 beta** (`"next-auth": "^5.0.0-beta.32"` — i.e. Auth.js v5), not the pages-router-era v4.
- `bcryptjs` (`^3.0.3`) for password hashing (credentials provider only).
- Providers configured: `Google` (OAuth) and `Credentials` (email/password).
- Session strategy: **JWT** (`session: { strategy: "jwt" }`), not database sessions.
- Custom sign-in page path set via `pages: { signIn: "/signin" }`.
- No adapter is configured (no `@auth/prisma-adapter` etc.) — consistent with JWT sessions and no DB.

---

## 3. Database / storage layer

- **None.** No Prisma, no SQL, no ORM, no JSON file persistence.
- `src/lib/users.ts` holds an **in-memory array**, pinned to `globalThis.__notelyUsers` to survive Turbopack's dual module instantiation in dev. This does **not** persist across server restarts and won't work across multiple serverless instances in production.
- "Schema" (informal, just the TS type):
```ts
type User = {
  id: string;
  email: string;
  name: string;
  createdAt: string;      // ISO date string, e.g. "2026-01-04"
  passwordHash?: string;  // undefined for OAuth-only users
};
```
- Seed data: two hardcoded users (`ada@example.com`, `grace@example.com`), no password hash (so they can't sign in via credentials, only illustrate the "Team" list on `/dashboard`).

---

## 4. How sessions work

- **JWT-based sessions** (Auth.js default when `strategy: "jwt"`), stored in an encrypted cookie set by NextAuth. No server-side session store/database.
- `AUTH_SECRET` env var is used to sign/encrypt the JWT (see `.env.example`).
- `auth()` (exported from `src/auth.ts`) is called server-side in Server Components (`layout.tsx`, `dashboard/page.tsx`) and in the proxy/middleware to get `session`/`req.auth`.
- Client-side sign-in/out use `next-auth/react`'s `signIn()` / `signOut()` (see `signin/page.tsx`, `signup/page.tsx`, `sign-out-button.tsx`), which round-trip through the `/api/auth/[...nextauth]` route handlers.
- Current user lookup for app data (`getCurrentUser()` in `users.ts`) re-derives the user by looking up `session.user.email` in the in-memory store — i.e., the JWT only carries id/email/name, not full profile.

---

## 5. Route protection ("middleware")

Notably, this project uses **`src/proxy.ts`**, not `src/middleware.ts`. (Next.js 16 has renamed/introduced `proxy.ts` as the middleware-equivalent convention — confirm this still functions as edge middleware in this Next version, since it's unconventional and worth double-checking during migration.)

```ts
import { NextResponse } from "next/server";
import { auth } from "@/auth";

export default auth((req) => {
  const isLoggedIn = !!req.auth;

  if (!isLoggedIn) {
    const signInUrl = new URL("/signin", req.nextUrl.origin);
    signInUrl.searchParams.set("callbackUrl", req.nextUrl.pathname);
    return NextResponse.redirect(signInUrl);
  }
});

export const config = {
  matcher: ["/dashboard/:path*"],
};
```
Only `/dashboard/*` is protected. If adding `/account`, it will need to be added to this matcher (or to whatever replaces it, e.g. Clerk's `clerkMiddleware`/`createRouteMatcher`).

---

